---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumb from '../components/Breadcrumb.astro';

const updated = 'October 11, 2026';
---
<Base
  title="Privacy & cookies"
  description="What MoodSupplement collects, why, how long we keep it, and how to change your cookie choice."
>
  <Breadcrumb crumbs={[
    { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
    { label: 'Privacy & cookies' },
  ]} />
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 pt-6 pb-16">
    <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Trust</p>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl sm:text-4xl text-moss-deep">Privacy &amp; cookies</h1>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-ink-soft">Last updated {updated}</p>
    <div class="prose-mr mt-6">
      <p>
        Short version: we run Google Analytics to see which articles get read, and nothing else.
        We do not sell data, we do not run ad networks, and we do not build profiles. If you are in
        the EEA, the UK or Switzerland, analytics stays switched off until you say yes.
      </p>

      <h2>Who we are</h2>
      <p>
        MoodSupplement (moodsupplement.net) is the data controller for this site. Reach us at
        <a href="mailto:hello@moodsupplement.net">hello@moodsupplement.net</a> or through our
        <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Change your cookie choice</h2>
      <p>
        Use the <button type="button" data-cc="show-preferencesModal" class="underline underline-offset-2">Cookie settings</button>
        link, here or in the footer of every page. Withdrawing consent is one click, exactly like
        giving it, and takes effect immediately.
      </p>

      <h2>Cookies and similar storage</h2>
      <h3>Strictly necessary</h3>
      <p>These are set no matter what, because without them the site cannot do its job. No consent is required for them, and none of them identify you.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>theme</strong>: browser local storage, not a cookie. Remembers light or dark mode. Kept until you clear your browser.</li>
        <li><strong>cc_cookie</strong>: your answer to the cookie banner, so we stop asking. Six months.</li>
        <li><strong>ms_geo</strong>: one of two values, <code>eu</code> or <code>row</code>, so we know whether to show you the banner. Derived from your IP address at our hosting edge and immediately discarded; the country itself is never stored. One day.</li>
        <li><strong>ms_op</strong>: set only if you visit a link ending <code>?ms_op=1</code>. Marks a browser as ours so our own visits do not pollute the stats.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Analytics (your choice)</h3>
      <p>
        Google Analytics 4, loaded through Google Tag Manager. It tells us which pages get read,
        roughly where readers come from, and which links get clicked. Aggregate reporting only.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>_ga, _ga_*</strong>: distinguish one browser from another and hold session state. Two years.</li>
        <li><strong>ms_src, ms_ref</strong>: session storage, not cookies. Record how this visit started (search, an AI assistant, a social app, direct) so we can tell those channels apart. Cleared when you close the tab.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        We use Google Consent Mode v2. If you decline, or before you answer, Google receives a
        cookieless signal that a page was viewed and nothing is written to your device. That signal
        carries no identifier and cannot be linked to you across visits or sites. Ad storage and ad
        personalisation are denied for everyone, everywhere, always, because we do not run ads.
      </p>

      <h2>What else we collect</h2>
      <h3>When you click a product link</h3>
      <p>
        Product buttons route through <code>/go/</code> on our own domain before forwarding you to
        the retailer. We log the product, where on the page you clicked, the channel that brought
        you to the site, your browser's user-agent string, and the referring page. We do not log IP
        addresses and we set no cookie for this. The purpose is knowing which recommendations are
        useful; the legal basis is our legitimate interest in understanding that, and the data
        cannot be used to single you out.
      </p>
      <p>
        Once you land on Amazon, a brand's own store, or Rakuten Advertising, you are on their site
        under their privacy policy, and they will set their own cookies, including the affiliate
        cookie that credits us for a purchase. We never see your order, your name, or your payment
        details. See our <a href="/disclosure/">affiliate disclosure</a> for how that arrangement works.
      </p>

      <h3>When you email us or sign up</h3>
      <p>
        The contact form and the cheat-sheet signup are processed by Netlify Forms. We receive what
        you type: your name and email on the contact form, your email on the signup. We use it to
        reply to you, or to send you the occasional email you asked for, and for nothing else. Ask
        us to delete it and we will. Every email we send carries an unsubscribe link.
      </p>

      <h3>Server logs</h3>
      <p>
        Our host, Netlify, keeps standard access logs (IP address, timestamp, requested URL,
        user-agent) for security and troubleshooting. That is Netlify's processing under their own
        retention schedule; we do not use those logs for analytics.
      </p>

      <h2>Who your data reaches</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Google</strong> (Ireland/US): analytics, if you consent.</li>
        <li><strong>Netlify</strong> (US): hosting, forms, and our click log.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Both are US-based, so analytics and form data may be processed outside the EEA. Transfers
        rely on the EU-US Data Privacy Framework and, where that does not apply, Standard
        Contractual Clauses.
      </p>

      <h2>How long we keep things</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Analytics data in GA4: 14 months, then deleted automatically.</li>
        <li>Click log: 13 months.</li>
        <li>Form submissions and email replies: until you ask us to delete them, or three years after our last exchange.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Your rights</h2>
      <p>
        If the GDPR or UK GDPR applies to you, you can ask us for a copy of your data, ask us to
        correct or delete it, object to processing based on legitimate interest, or ask us to
        restrict it. Email <a href="mailto:hello@moodsupplement.net">hello@moodsupplement.net</a>
        and we will answer within 30 days. You can also complain to your national data protection
        authority. California residents have comparable rights under the CCPA/CPRA, including the
        right to know and to delete; we do not sell or share personal information, so there is no
        opt-out to exercise.
      </p>
      <p>
        One honest caveat: analytics data is not tied to your name, so for most requests we have no
        way to find "your" rows and nothing to hand over. Deleting the cookies in your browser,
        or declining analytics, is the effective remedy there.
      </p>

      <h2>Children</h2>
      <p>This site is not aimed at anyone under 16 and we do not knowingly collect their data.</p>

      <h2>Health information</h2>
      <p>
        We do not ask about your health and you should not send us health details. Reading an
        article about a supplement tells our analytics that a page was viewed, not anything about
        you. If you email us something personal, we keep it to the conversation.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>
        If we change what we collect, the date at the top of this page moves and the banner asks
        again where consent is needed.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
